<div>
    <div class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-orange-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Cloudflare Workers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            Run a Soli app inside a Cloudflare Worker: the interpreter compiled to WebAssembly, your app's source shipped with it, and each request answered by the same routing, controllers, views and models <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> runs &mdash; next to your visitors, with no server to keep up.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-8 p-6 rounded-xl bg-green-500/10 border border-green-500/20 mb-12">
        <h4 class="text-lg font-semibold text-green-400 mb-1">Live example</h4>
        <p class="text-green-200/80 text-sm">
            <a href="https://cf.solisoft.net" class="underline">cf.solisoft.net</a> is a Soli app running this way. It is <code>examples/cloudflare-worker</code> in the Soli repository, and it explains this page in pages of its own.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-12">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">./scripts/build-edge.sh                      # once per Soli version → target/edge
soli edge build . --runtime target/edge      # your app → dist/edge
cd dist/edge &amp;&amp; npx wrangler dev             # on workerd, locally
npx wrangler deploy                          # on Cloudflare</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h2 id="new" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Start an app for Workers: <code>soli new --edge</code></h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli new my_app --edge
cd my_app && soli edge dev       # on workerd, http://localhost:8787</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">The usual application, set up to deploy as a Worker:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code>wrangler.toml</code></strong> beside <code>app/</code> &mdash; the Worker's name, its static assets, models on D1 (<code>SOLI_DB_ADAPTER = "d1"</code>), sessions in the <code>cookie</code> driver, and the R2, Images, Queues and Hyperdrive blocks ready to uncomment. <code>soli edge build</code> copies it in on every build: commit it.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code>.env</code></strong> for <code>soli serve</code> on your machine: the same models on SQLite (D1 is SQLite) and the same cookie sessions, with a generated <code>SOLI_SESSION_SECRET</code>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code>.dev.vars</code></strong> &mdash; the same secret for <code>soli edge dev</code> (wrangler's local-only variables); on Cloudflare it is set with <code>npx wrangler secret put</code>.</li>
        <li><code>.gitignore</code> entries for <code>dist/edge/</code>, <code>.wrangler/</code> and <code>.dev.vars</code>, and a README and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> that say where the app runs and how to deploy it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-it-works" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">How it works</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">The edge runtime</strong> is the Soli library built for <code class="text-amber-400">wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> without its server, CLI and native-only builtins: about 11.7&nbsp;MB of WebAssembly, 3.6&nbsp;MB gzipped. <code class="text-amber-400">scripts/build-edge.sh</code> builds it from the <code class="text-amber-400">edge/</code> crate.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-amber-400">soli edge build</code></strong> writes a plain Wrangler project: the runtime, a small JavaScript host (<code class="text-amber-400">src/worker.js</code>), your app's source as <code class="text-amber-400">src/app.json</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">public/</code> as the Worker's static assets.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">At the first request</strong> an isolate instantiates the runtime, mounts the app's files in memory (a Worker has no filesystem) and boots the app the way a <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> worker does: a few tens of milliseconds, once per isolate. Later requests take a few milliseconds.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Every request</strong> goes through <code class="text-amber-400">handle_request</code>, the function <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code>'s workers call: middleware, CSRF, routing, the action on the VM, the view and its layout.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Model queries</strong> go to Cloudflare D1 through the Worker's binding, to PostgreSQL through a JavaScript driver (usually via Hyperdrive), or to SoliDB over HTTP(S) with <code class="text-amber-400">fetch</code>. The interpreter is synchronous and they are asynchronous, so the wasm stack <em>suspends</em> on each query &mdash; <a href="https://v8.dev/blog/jspi" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">JavaScript Promise Integration</a> &mdash; and resumes when the answer arrives. Your code does not change.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="runtime" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Build the edge runtime</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">Once per Soli version, in the Soli source tree:</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown
cargo install wasm-bindgen-cli --version 0.2.126 --locked
./scripts/build-edge.sh</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The wasm-bindgen CLI must match the version <code class="text-amber-400">edge/Cargo.toml</code> pins; the script checks and prints the install line when it does not. Nothing in the wasm build compiles C. The output, in <code class="text-amber-400">target/edge</code> (or <code class="text-amber-400">$OUT</code>):
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-12">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead class="text-gray-300 border-b border-white/10"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">File</th><th class="py-3 px-4">What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">soli_edge_bg.wasm</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the interpreter (opt-level <code>z</code>, fat LTO)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">soli_edge.js</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">wasm-bindgen glue</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">worker.js</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the Worker: boots the app, queues requests into wasm</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">jspi.js</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the one import the stack suspends on</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="package" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Package an app: <code>soli edge build</code></h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli edge build [folder] [--out DIR] [--runtime DIR] [--sql postgres|none]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead class="text-gray-300 border-b border-white/10"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Option</th><th class="py-3 px-4">Default</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">folder</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>.</code> &mdash; the app (it must have <code>app/controllers</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">--out</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">-o</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>&lt;folder&gt;/dist/edge</code> &mdash; the Wrangler project to write</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">--runtime</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>$SOLI_EDGE_RUNTIME</code>, then <code>edge/</code> or <code>../share/soli/edge</code> next to the <code>soli</code> binary</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">--sql</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">detected &mdash; <code>postgres</code> to bundle its driver, <code>none</code> for nothing (see <a href="#hyperdrive" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">PostgreSQL</a>)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><code class="text-amber-400">app/</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">config/</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">lib/</code> go into <code>src/app.json</code>, mounted at <code>/app</code> in the Worker. Only UTF-8 text files: a binary file is skipped with a line saying so (binary files belong in <code>public/</code>).</li>
        <li><code class="text-amber-400">public/</code> is copied fresh each build and served as <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">static assets</a>, before the Worker runs.</li>
        <li><code class="text-amber-400">.env</code> and anything outside those directories is <strong class="text-white">not</strong> bundled.</li>
        <li><code class="text-amber-400">wrangler.toml</code> is written on the first build only: later builds keep your name, routes, vars and account. An app that keeps its <strong class="text-white">own</strong> <code>wrangler.toml</code> next to <code>app/</code> has it copied in on every build instead: commit it, and the deploy config lives with the code (nothing in it is a secret; tokens come from <code>wrangler login</code> or <code>CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN</code>). Add <code>dist/</code> to the app's <code>.gitignore</code>.</li>
        <li>For an app on PostgreSQL, the driver: <code>src/sql-pg.js</code>, <code>pg</code> in <code>package.json</code>, and <code>npm install</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="dev" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Develop on workerd: <code>soli edge dev</code></h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli edge dev [folder] [--port 8787] [--runtime DIR] [--sql postgres|none]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Packages the app into <code>dist/edge</code>, runs it with <code>wrangler dev --local</code> &mdash; the runtime Cloudflare runs, with local D1, R2, KV and Queues stored under <code>dist/edge/.wrangler/state</code> &mdash; and repackages on every change under <code>app/</code>, <code>config/</code>, <code>lib/</code>, <code>public/</code> or to <code>wrangler.toml</code>; wrangler reloads the Worker and the open page. The app's <code>.dev.vars</code> (wrangler's local-only variables) is copied in.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code>wrangler dev</code> hands the Worker the production host of <code>routes</code> as <code>Host</code>, so <code>soli edge dev</code> adds the addresses the page is really opened from to <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_APP_HOSTS</code> &mdash; <code>localhost:&lt;port&gt;</code>, <code>127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code>, and the hosts listed in the app's <code>.env</code> or passed by soli-proxy &mdash; or forms would fail the same-origin check locally.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Behind <strong class="text-white">soli-proxy</strong> it is a site like the others (<code>sites/&lt;name&gt;</code> linking to the app, with an <code>app.infos</code>):</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="app.infos"><code class="language-toml text-sm">name = "cf.solisoft.test"
domain = "cf.solisoft.test"
start_script = "env SOLI_EDGE_RUNTIME=/path/to/edge-runtime soli edge dev . --port $PORT"
health_check = "/"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12"><code>https://cf.solisoft.test</code> then shows the page the Worker renders.</p>

    <h2 id="configure" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Configure</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Everything an app reads with <code class="text-amber-400">getenv()</code> comes from the Worker's bindings: plain values under <code class="text-amber-400">[vars]</code> in <code class="text-amber-400">wrangler.toml</code>, secrets with Wrangler.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[vars]
SOLIDB_HOST = "https://db.example.com"
SOLIDB_DATABASE = "my_app"
SOLIDB_USERNAME = "app"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">npx wrangler secret put SOLIDB_PASSWORD</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        The runtime also sets <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_RUNTIME</code> (<code>"cloudflare-workers"</code>) and <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_VERSION</code>, unless the Worker does. Request headers carry what Cloudflare knows about the visitor: <code class="text-amber-400">cf-connecting-ip</code> (also the request's peer IP), <code class="text-amber-400">cf-ipcountry</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">cf-colo</code> &mdash; the datacenter that ran the request, added by <code>worker.js</code> from <code>request.cf.colo</code>. When the edge sent no <code class="text-amber-400">cf-ipcountry</code> (IP geolocation off on the zone, or <code>wrangler dev</code>, which fills <code>request.cf</code> but adds no <code>cf-*</code> header), <code>worker.js</code> adds it from <code>request.cf.country</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="deploy" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Deploy</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">npx wrangler deploy</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        For your own domain, once its zone is on the same Cloudflare account, add to <code class="text-amber-400">wrangler.toml</code> &mdash; above the <code>[assets]</code> table, or TOML files it under it:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">routes = [{ pattern = "app.example.com", custom_domain = true }]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        A Wrangler API token needs <em>Workers Scripts: Edit</em> on the account, and <em>Workers Routes: Edit</em> on the zone for a custom domain. Wrangler finds the account from the token; with access to several, set <code class="text-amber-400">account_id</code> in <code>wrangler.toml</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="databases" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Databases</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Models work as they do under <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> &mdash; <code>find</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>count</code>, aggregates, <code>update_all</code> / <code>delete_all</code>, validations, callbacks &mdash; on D1, PostgreSQL or SoliDB.
    </p>

    <h3 id="d1" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Cloudflare D1</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/d1/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">D1</a> is SQLite run by Cloudflare, next to the Worker: an app with D1 needs no database server of its own.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">npx wrangler d1 create my-app-db        # prints the database_id</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[[d1_databases]]
binding = "DB"
database_name = "my-app-db"
database_id = "…"

[vars]
SOLI_DB_ADAPTER = "d1"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_DB_ADAPTER = "d1"</code> puts models on the D1 adapter, and <code class="text-amber-400">DATABASE_URL</code> names the binding: <code>d1://DB</code>, which is the default. Under <code>wrangler dev</code> the database is local (<code>.wrangler/state</code>); <code>npx wrangler d1 execute my-app-db --local --command "SELECT …"</code> reads it. In <code>config/database.toml</code>, a D1 connection is <code>adapter = "d1"</code>, <code>url = "d1://BINDING"</code>.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        It is the SQLite adapter's document model &mdash; a table per model with a <code>_key</code> and a JSON <code>doc</code> column, created on first write &mdash; and the SQLite adapter's SQL, sent to the binding with <code>prepare(…).bind(…).all()</code>. Each statement is a round trip, so writes use <code>RETURNING doc</code> rather than reading the row back, and the reads inside a <a href="/docs/database/query-builder#grouped" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline"><code>grouped(fn() { … })</code></a> block go to D1 together in one <code>batch()</code>: one round trip for the block. What D1 does not offer:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-10">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Transactions.</strong> D1 runs batches, not interactive transactions: <code>Model.transaction</code> raises.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Column-aware models</strong> (a model that declares its columns) raise: store them as documents.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">The job table and cron</strong>: on the edge, jobs go through <a href="#queues" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cloudflare Queues</a> and <code>static cron</code> through <a href="#cron" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cron Triggers</a> instead.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-10">
        One D1 database per site: a connection <code>url = "d1://SITE_{tenant}"</code> sends each tenant's queries to its own binding (<code>switch_tenant("acme")</code> &rarr; <code>SITE_acme</code>), declared in <code>wrangler.toml</code> with one <code>[[d1_databases]]</code> block per site. Bindings are fixed at deploy, so a new site means a new binding and a redeploy &mdash; for many sites, use <a href="#durable-objects" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">SQLite in Durable Objects</a> instead. See <a href="/docs/database/multi-database#tenants" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">one database per tenant</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="durable-objects" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">SQLite in Durable Objects: a database per tenant</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        A <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Durable Object</a> with SQLite storage is a small database of its own, created the first time it is named. Soli's D1 adapter reaches one with a <code class="text-amber-400">do://</code> url: the binding, then the object's name.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="config/database.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">default = "primary"

[connections.primary]            # accounts, sites — on D1
adapter = "d1"
url = "d1://DB"

[connections.site]               # each site's data — its own Durable Object
adapter = "d1"
url = "do://SITES/{tenant}"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[[durable_objects.bindings]]
name = "SITES"
class_name = "SoliSqlObject"

[[migrations]]
tag = "v1"
new_sqlite_classes = ["SoliSqlObject"]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        <code class="text-amber-400">SoliSqlObject</code> ships in every Worker <code>soli edge build</code> packages: it runs the statements it is sent on its own storage and answers like D1, so models, <code>grouped</code> (one RPC per block, run in one storage transaction), and everything else the D1 adapter does work unchanged. With <a href="/docs/database/multi-database#tenants" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline"><code>switch_tenant(site.db_key)</code></a> in a middleware, <code>do://SITES/{tenant}</code> gives every site a database of its own &mdash; unlike a D1 binding per site, nothing is declared per site and nothing is redeployed when one is added. A fixed name (<code>do://SITES/main</code>) makes a single object-backed database.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-10">
        The same limits as D1 apply (no <code>Model.transaction</code>, no column-aware models), a Durable Object holds up to 10&nbsp;GB, and its object name is limited to letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>. Under <code>soli edge dev</code> the objects are local, in <code>dist/edge/.wrangler/state</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="solidb" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">SoliDB</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Over SoliDB's HTTP API. The Worker must be able to reach <code class="text-amber-400">SOLIDB_HOST</code>: a public <code>https://</code> address. Credentials follow the usual order (JWT login with <code>SOLIDB_USERNAME</code> / <code>SOLIDB_PASSWORD</code>, <code>SOLIDB_API_KEY</code>, then basic auth).
    </p>

    <h3 id="hyperdrive" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">PostgreSQL (Hyperdrive)</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        A Worker has no socket a Rust client could open, so the edge build does not carry the native <code>postgres</code> client. It sends the SQL that adapter compiles to a JavaScript driver in the Worker, <code>pg</code>, which connects through the Worker's TCP sockets. <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/hyperdrive/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Hyperdrive</a> keeps a pool of connections next to the database for it, so a request does not pay a TLS handshake to the database.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">npx wrangler hyperdrive create my-db --connection-string="postgres://user:password@db.example.com:5432/my_app"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">compatibility_flags = ["nodejs_compat"]   # top level, above the first table

[[hyperdrive]]
binding = "HYPERDRIVE"
id = "…"                                  # what `hyperdrive create` printed
localConnectionString = "postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/my_app"

[vars]
SOLI_DB_ADAPTER = "postgres"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code class="text-amber-400">DATABASE_URL</code> defaults to <code>hyperdrive://HYPERDRIVE</code>; <code>hyperdrive://NAME</code> names another binding, and a plain <code>postgres://</code> URL is connected to directly, without Hyperdrive. <code>wrangler dev</code> uses <code>localConnectionString</code> (it must carry a password).
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code class="text-amber-400">soli edge build</code> bundles the driver when the app's configuration names Postgres &mdash; <code>adapter = "postgres"</code> in <code>config/database.toml</code>, <code>SOLI_DB_ADAPTER</code> in <code>.env</code> or under <code>[vars]</code> &mdash; or when told with <code>--sql postgres</code> (<code>--sql none</code> bundles nothing). It copies the driver's glue, adds <code>pg</code> to <code>package.json</code>, runs <code>npm install</code>, and puts <code>nodejs_compat</code> in a new <code>wrangler.toml</code>; an existing one without it earns a warning.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The models are the native adapter's: the same document tables and SQL, column-aware models (<code>table "orders"</code>) included, and <strong class="text-white">transactions</strong>, since every statement of a request runs on one connection &mdash; opened at the request's first query, closed (handed back to Hyperdrive's pool) when it ends. What stays with <code>soli serve</code>: <code>db:create</code> / <code>db:drop</code> and schema dumps (jobs run through <a href="#queues" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cloudflare Queues</a>, <code>static cron</code> through <a href="#cron" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cron Triggers</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Not available on the edge: <strong class="text-white">MySQL</strong> (no driver glue yet), SQLite and the native SoliDB driver. The edge build uses D1, PostgreSQL or SoliDB over HTTP.
    </p>

    <h2 id="files" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Files: uploads, images and video</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Files in <code>public/</code> are Cloudflare static assets: images and video served before the Worker runs, <code>Range</code> included.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Uploads work as under <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> &mdash; a multipart form reaches the action, and <code>find_uploaded_file</code> / <code>attach_&lt;field&gt;</code> take the file &mdash; and they are kept in <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">R2</a>, Cloudflare's object storage, with <code class="text-amber-400">service: "r2"</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="app/models/photo.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class Photo &lt; Model
  has_one_attached("image", {
    "service": "r2",
    "content_types": ["image/jpeg", "image/png", "video/mp4"],
    "max_size": 20_000_000
  })
end</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">npx wrangler r2 bucket create my-app-files</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[[r2_buckets]]
binding = "ATTACHMENTS"
bucket_name = "my-app-files"

# Image transforms (optional).
[images]
binding = "IMAGES"</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The Worker talks to the bucket through its binding: no keys to keep. <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_ATTACHMENTS_R2_BINDING</code> names another binding than <code>ATTACHMENTS</code>. Under <code>soli serve</code> the same declaration uses R2's S3-compatible API, with the <code>s3</code> service's configuration &mdash; <code>S3_ENDPOINT=https://&lt;account&gt;.r2.cloudflarestorage.com</code>, <code>S3_REGION=auto</code>, an R2 API token's <code>S3_ACCESS_KEY</code> / <code>S3_SECRET_KEY</code>, and <code>SOLI_ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET</code> &mdash; so a local app and its Worker read the same objects, under the same keys (<code>&lt;collection&gt;/&lt;blob id&gt;</code>).
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <strong class="text-white">Reading back never crosses wasm.</strong> The blob route (<code>&lt;field&gt;_url</code>) checks the record and sets its headers, then hands the object to <code>worker.js</code>, which answers from the bucket. A video player that seeks gets <code>206 Partial Content</code> with just the bytes it asked for, a revalidation gets <code>304</code>, and memory stays flat whatever the file's size.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <strong class="text-white">Image transforms</strong> &mdash; <code>photo_url({"w": 400, "fmt": "webp"})</code>, <code>?thumb=</code>, <code>?square=</code>, <code>?fit=cover</code>, <code>?crop=</code>, <code>?rot=</code>, <code>?gray=1</code>, <code>?q=</code> &mdash; are run by the <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/transform-images/bindings/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Images binding</a>, WebP and AVIF output included, on Cloudflare's machines rather than in the Worker's CPU budget. Without an <code>[images]</code> binding they answer the stored bytes. Images has no hue rotation or inversion, so <code>hue</code> and <code>invert</code> are ignored there, and <code>blur</code>, <code>bright</code> and <code>contrast</code> are approximations of what <code>soli serve</code> computes. Images bills transformations beyond its free monthly allowance.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        The Worker itself carries no image codecs: they would add 260&nbsp;KB (gzipped) to it for work Cloudflare Images does better. On the edge the <code>Image</code> class raises, saying so, and an uploader's storage-time transform (<code>format</code>, <code>max_width</code>, <code>max_height</code>) is skipped: the original is stored, and URL transforms resize it on read. What uploads cannot do on the edge:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Large files.</strong> The body arrives whole, and a file is copied a few times on its way into R2 (the request, wasm memory, the parsed part, its base64 form), against an isolate limit of 128&nbsp;MB: keep <code>max_size</code> to about 20&nbsp;MB.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Resumable (tus) uploads and <code>direct_upload</code></strong>, which need a disk or S3 credentials.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="queues" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Background jobs: Cloudflare Queues</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        A Worker has no thread to poll a job table with, but Cloudflare runs a Worker's <code>queue()</code> handler for each batch of messages a <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Queue</a> receives. So on the edge, <code class="text-amber-400">perform_later</code>, <code>perform_in</code>, <code>perform_at</code>, <code>Job.enqueue*</code> and <code>Webhook.enqueue*</code> send the job &mdash; the row the native engine would insert &mdash; as a message, and the Worker runs it: the same job classes from <code>app/jobs/</code>, the same <code>static def perform(args)</code>, no code change.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">npx wrangler queues create my-app-jobs</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[[queues.producers]]
binding = "JOBS"
queue = "my-app-jobs"

[[queues.consumers]]
queue = "my-app-jobs"
max_retries = 100        # Soli decides retries with each job's max_retries</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A failed job is redelivered after the native engine's backoff (5&nbsp;s, 10&nbsp;s, 20&nbsp;s&hellip; capped at an hour) until its <code>max_retries</code> is spent, then acknowledged and logged as dead (<code>npx wrangler tail</code> shows it). Delivery is at least once, as with the job table: keep <code>perform</code> idempotent. All Soli queue names travel in one Cloudflare Queue; <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_QUEUE_BINDING</code> names another producer binding than <code>JOBS</code>. A job runs in turn with the requests of its isolate, under the same CPU limit. What the edge cannot do with them:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Delays past 12 hours</strong>, Cloudflare's limit: <code>perform_in("1 day", …)</code> raises.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Row operations</strong> &mdash; <code>Job.list</code>, <code>Job.queues</code>, <code>Job.cancel</code>, <code>Job.retry</code> &mdash; raise: Cloudflare does not hand messages back. Its dashboard shows the queue.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Cron</strong>: <code>static cron</code> runs on <a href="#cron" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cron Triggers</a>; <code>Cron.schedule</code> and the other <code>Cron.*</code> calls that write the cron table do not run.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="cron" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Cron: Cloudflare Cron Triggers</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        A job class&rsquo;s <code>static cron</code> runs on the edge with no change: Cloudflare calls the Worker&rsquo;s <code>scheduled()</code> handler at each time <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/cron-triggers/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline"><code>[triggers] crons</code></a> in <code>wrangler.toml</code> lists, and the Worker runs the jobs whose schedule it is.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-4">
        <pre data-filename="app/jobs/nightly_report_job.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">class NightlyReportJob
  static cron = Cron.daily_at("03:15")

  static def perform(args)
    Report.generate
  end
end</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><code>soli edge build</code> reads the <code>static cron</code> declarations of <code>app/jobs/</code> and writes the triggers into the <code>wrangler.toml</code> it generates; an existing <code>wrangler.toml</code> gets a warning that lists the ones it lacks:</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[triggers]
crons = ["15 3 * * *"]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Five fields, UTC, to the minute.</strong> Soli&rsquo;s expressions have a seconds field first (<code>0 15 3 * * *</code>); a trigger drops it. A schedule that needs seconds (<code>Cron.every("30 seconds")</code>, <code>"30 * * * * *"</code>) fails the build, naming its file.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Read from the source.</strong> A string literal or a <code>Cron.*</code> helper called with literals (<code>daily_at</code>, <code>hourly</code>, <code>every</code>, <code>weekly_at</code>). A schedule computed when the app runs (a constant) is a warning: add its trigger by hand.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Several jobs on one schedule share a trigger</strong> and all run, one after the other, with no tenant. One failing does not stop the others; it fails the invocation, so Cloudflare&rsquo;s dashboard shows it, and <code>npx wrangler tail</code> its error. There are no retries: a job that must not be lost can enqueue its work with <code>perform_later</code> (Cloudflare Queues retry).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Not available:</strong> <code>Cron.schedule</code> / <code>update</code> / <code>delete</code> and <code>Cron.list</code>, which write and read the cron table: a trigger is fixed when the Worker is deployed.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Count.</strong> Cloudflare allows 5 Cron Triggers per account on the Workers Free plan, 250 on Paid; the build warns past 5.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Locally</strong>, <code>npx wrangler dev --test-scheduled</code> and <code>curl "http://127.0.0.1:8787/__scheduled?cron=15+3+*+*+*"</code> fire a trigger. <a href="https://cf.solisoft.net/d1" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">cf.solisoft.net/d1</a> shows the last run of an hourly job.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="mail" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Mail: over HTTP</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A Worker cannot open a connection to a mail server, so <code>delivery_method: "smtp"</code> raises there, saying so. Mail goes out over HTTP instead: the <a href="/docs/builtins/mailer#http" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline"><code>http</code> delivery method</a> POSTs each message to a provider&rsquo;s API &mdash; as JSON, or reshaped by a <a href="/docs/blog/mail-over-http-provider-classes" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">provider class</a> (Brevo, Resend, Postmark, SendGrid, Mailgun) &mdash; through the Worker&rsquo;s <code>fetch</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="config/application.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">Mailer.configure({"delivery_method": "http", "request": "BrevoMail",
                  "api_key": getenv("BREVO_API_KEY"), "from": "Acme &lt;noreply@example.com&gt;"})</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Keep the key a secret (<code>npx wrangler secret put BREVO_API_KEY</code>). Your mailers do not change: <code>deliver_now</code> sends from the request, and <code>deliver_later</code> becomes a <a href="#queues" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cloudflare Queues</a> message whose job sends it, retried like any job &mdash; without a <code>JOBS</code> binding it sends at once. Attachments travel base64 in the request, so keep them well under the ~20&nbsp;MB a request body allows.
    </p>

    <h2 id="liveview" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">LiveView: sockets in Durable Objects</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A <a href="/docs/core-concepts/liveview" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">LiveView</a> runs on the edge unchanged: the same template, the same <code>router_live</code> handler, the same <code>/live/client.js</code>. A Worker cannot keep a socket between requests, so each LiveView instance gets a <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Durable Object</a>:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <li>The Worker sends <code>/live/socket/&lt;component&gt;</code> to the object of that instance &mdash; the visitor&rsquo;s session&rsquo;s, or the room&rsquo;s for a component <code>live_rooms</code> declared (an undeclared <code>?room=</code> shares nothing, as under <code>soli serve</code>).</li>
        <li>The object accepts the socket with the <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">hibernation API</a> and keeps the instance &mdash; its state and last markup &mdash; in its own storage. It can be evicted between events; the next event wakes it, reads the instance back, runs one frame (handler, render, diff) and stores the result. Every socket of the instance gets the patch.</li>
        <li><code>soli edge build</code> adds the binding to the <code>wrangler.toml</code> it writes when <code>config/routes.sl</code> declares <code>router_live</code>; an existing <code>wrangler.toml</code> gets a warning naming it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="rounded-xl bg-[#0C0A09] ring-1 ring-white/10 overflow-hidden shadow-xl mb-6">
        <pre data-filename="wrangler.toml"><code class="language-toml text-sm">[[durable_objects.bindings]]
name = "SOLI_LIVE"
class_name = "SoliLiveObject"

[[migrations]]
tag = "soli-live-v1"
new_sqlite_classes = ["SoliLiveObject"]</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Measured on <code>wrangler dev --local</code> (workerd) with a counter: a click takes about 1&nbsp;ms inside wasm and 8&ndash;10&nbsp;ms round trip; the first socket of a fresh isolate gets its render in 65&ndash;170&nbsp;ms (boot included). The instance survives a restart of the whole process. LiveView adds about 205&nbsp;KB to the runtime. <a href="https://cf.solisoft.net/live" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">cf.solisoft.net/live</a> is a shared board running this way.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Not carried on the edge yet: <code>tick</code> intervals, live queries (<code>Model.live_where</code>), <code>live_update</code> from a controller or a job &mdash; they need a process that stays up &mdash; LiveView uploads (<code>soli-upload</code>), and EUI. Other WebSocket routes and server-sent events still answer <code>501</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="scope" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">What runs on the edge</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead class="text-gray-300 border-b border-white/10"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Runs</th><th class="py-3 px-4">Stays with <code>soli serve</code></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">routes, controllers, <code>before_action</code>, middleware; <a href="#liveview" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">LiveView</a></td><td class="py-3 px-4">other WebSockets; LiveView <code>tick</code>, live queries, <code>live_update</code></td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">views, layouts, partials, components, helpers</td><td class="py-3 px-4">server-sent events, <code>stream</code>, blob streaming (answered <code>501</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">query strings, URL-encoded and multipart forms, JSON</td><td class="py-3 px-4">resumable (tus) and direct uploads, file writes</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">i18n from <code>config/locales</code>; background jobs through Cloudflare Queues; <code>static cron</code> through <a href="#cron" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Cron Triggers</a></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>Cron.schedule</code> and the cron table, SMTP (mail goes <a href="#mail" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">over HTTP</a>)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">models on D1, PostgreSQL (Hyperdrive), or SoliDB over HTTP(S)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">MySQL, SQLite, the native SoliDB driver; transactions on D1</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">the <code>HTTP</code> class (<code>fetch</code>); <code>HTTP.parallel*</code> run one after another</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>--dev</code>: hot reload, dev bar, REPL</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">static files from <code>public/</code>; attachments on R2, with <code>Range</code> and image transforms</td><td class="py-3 px-4">PDF, Office, the <code>Image</code> class, <code>System.run</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">A builtin that needs what a Worker lacks returns an error naming the edge build (&ldquo;&hellip; is not available on the edge (Cloudflare Workers) build&rdquo;).</p>

    <h2 id="limits" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Limits worth knowing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">One request at a time per isolate.</strong> A request suspended on a query keeps its isolate's wasm stack; <code>worker.js</code> queues the next one behind it. Cloudflare spreads load across isolates, but a slow query delays the requests queued in the same isolate.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">A Rust panic ends the isolate.</strong> wasm is <code>panic = abort</code>: where <code>soli serve</code> answers a 500 and keeps the worker, the edge build loses the isolate, and the next request boots a fresh one.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Size.</strong> About 11.7&nbsp;MB uncompressed (3.6&nbsp;MB gzipped), against Workers&rsquo; 64&nbsp;MiB limit on the uncompressed bundle, the same on the free and paid plans; there is no compressed limit any more. The Workers limit that still applies is startup time: 1&nbsp;second. The Postgres driver adds about 40&nbsp;KB.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Sessions.</strong> The default in-memory store lives as long as an isolate; use the <code>cookie</code> driver (<code>SOLI_SESSION_DRIVER=cookie</code> and a 32+ character <code>SOLI_SESSION_SECRET</code> secret).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Redirects</strong> of the <code>HTTP</code> class are followed by <code>fetch</code>; the SSRF check still screens every URL you pass it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="troubleshooting" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Troubleshooting</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5 mb-12">
        <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
            <thead class="text-gray-300 border-b border-white/10"><tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Symptom</th><th class="py-3 px-4">Cause</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-400">
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>Soli failed to boot: …</code> (500 on every request)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">the app did not load; the message is the boot error (syntax error, missing <code>app/controllers</code>…)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>501 Streaming responses are not available…</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the action streams (SSE, <code>stream</code>, <code>solidb_blob_response</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>… is not available on the edge (Cloudflare Workers) build</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">a builtin that needs a socket, a thread, a subprocess or a disk</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>no edge runtime found</code> from <code>soli edge build</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">pass <code>--runtime</code> or set <code>SOLI_EDGE_RUNTIME</code> to the directory <code>build-edge.sh</code> wrote</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4">a model call hangs, then the request fails</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SOLIDB_HOST</code> is not reachable from Cloudflare (a LAN address, <code>localhost</code>)</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>no D1 binding named DB</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>wrangler.toml</code> lacks the <code>[[d1_databases]]</code> block whose <code>binding</code> the D1 url names</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>… is not supported on Cloudflare D1</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">a transaction, a column-aware model, or the job table / cron on a D1 connection</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>no Hyperdrive binding named HYPERDRIVE</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>wrangler.toml</code> lacks the <code>[[hyperdrive]]</code> block the connection's url names</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>this Worker carries no postgres driver</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the build did not see the dialect: <code>soli edge build --sql postgres</code></td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>no Queue binding named JOBS</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>wrangler.toml</code> lacks the <code>[[queues.producers]]</code> block for the job queue</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>MySQL is not available on the edge build</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the app's connection is MySQL: move it to Postgres (Hyperdrive), D1 or SoliDB</td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>CSRF check failed: Origin … does not match</code> (403)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">a POST whose Origin is another site &mdash; the same-origin gate of <code>soli serve</code>; list your other public hostnames in <code>SOLI_APP_HOSTS</code></td></tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/5"><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>no R2 binding named ATTACHMENTS</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>wrangler.toml</code> lacks the <code>[[r2_buckets]]</code> block an <code>r2</code> attachment needs</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">an image URL with <code>?w=</code> answers the full-size original</td><td class="py-3 px-4">no <code>[images]</code> binding in <code>wrangler.toml</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        For servers you run yourself, see <a href="/docs/development-tools/deploy" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Deploy</a>.
    </p>
</div>
